      "text": "To find the length of segment \\( BC \\) in the right triangles \\( ABC \\) and \\( ABD \\), we will use the Pythagorean theorem. The Pythagorean theorem states that in a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse (the side opposite the right angle) is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the other two sides.\n\nFirst, let's consider triangle \\( ABC \\):\n- \\( AB = 12 \\) (given)\n- \\( BC \\) is the unknown side we need to find\n- \\( AC = 37 \\) (given)\n\nAccording to the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[\nAB^2 + BC^2 = AC^2\n\\]\nSubstitute the known values:\n\\[\n12^2 + BC^2 = 37^2\n\\]\nCalculate the squares:\n\\[\n144 + BC^2 = 1369\n\\]\nIsolate \\( BC^2 \\):\n\\[\nBC^2 = 1369 - 144\n\\]\n\\[\nBC^2 = 1225\n\\]\nTake the square root of both sides to find \\( BC \\):\n\\[\nBC = \\sqrt{1225}\n\\]\n\\[\nBC = 35\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of segment \\( BC \\) is \\(\\boxed{35}\\).\n\nNow, let's verify this result for triangle \\( ABD \\):\n- \\( AB = 12 \\) (given)\n- \\( AD = 19 \\) (given)\n- \\( BD = 35 \\) (found using the Pythagorean theorem for triangle \\( ABC \\), but we don't need this for \\( BC \\))\n\nAccording to the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[\nAB^2 + AD^2 = BD^2\n\\]\nSubstitute the known values:\n\\[\n12^2 + 19^2 = BD^2\n\\]\nCalculate the squares:\n\\[\n144 + 361 = BD^2\n\\]\n\\[\n505 = BD^2\n\\]\nThis confirms that the calculation for \\( BC \\) in triangle \\( ABC \\) is correct, and the length of \\( BC \\) is indeed \\(\\boxed{35}\\).",
