      "text": "To solve for the length of segment \\( BC \\) in the given right triangles \\( ABC \\) and \\( ABD \\), we can use the Pythagorean theorem in both triangles.\n\nFirst, let's consider triangle \\( ABC \\). Since \\( \\angle ABC = 90^\\circ \\), triangle \\( ABC \\) is a right triangle. We know the lengths of two sides: \\( AB = 12 \\) units and \\( AC = 16 \\) units. We need to find the length of \\( BC \\).\n\nUsing the Pythagorean theorem for triangle \\( ABC \\):\n\\[\nAB^2 + BC^2 = AC^2\n\\]\nSubstitute the known values:\n\\[\n12^2 + BC^2 = 16^2\n\\]\nCalculate the squares:\n\\[\n144 + BC^2 = 256\n\\]\nIsolate \\( BC^2 \\):\n\\[\nBC^2 = 256 - 144\n\\]\n\\[\nBC^2 = 112\n\\]\nTake the square root of both sides:\n\\[\nBC = \\sqrt{112} = \\sqrt{16 \\times 7} = 4\\sqrt{7}\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of segment \\( BC \\) is \\(\\boxed{4\\sqrt{7}}\\) units.",
